  I'd spec stage one next and keep stage two's needs as explicit constraints on it, so we don't build a write path that
  can't emit events.

  [1mTables.[0m

  users            id, name
  tasks            id, title, description, status,
                   creator_id, assignee_id, created_at, updated_at
  comments         id, task_id, author_id, body, created_at
  task_events      id, task_id, actor_id, type, payload, created_at
  task_participants  task_id, user_id, reason        -- (stage 1)
  notification_state user_id, read_cursor_event_id   -- (stage 2)
  dismissed_events   user_id, event_id               -- (stage 2)

  [38;5;153mtask_events.type[39m is one of [38;5;153mcreated[39m, [38;5;153mstatus_changed[39m, [38;5;153massigned[39m, [38;5;153mcommented[39m. Field edits deliberately emit nothing.

  [1mThe one non-obvious choice: [38;5;153mtask_participants[39m is materialized, not derived.[0m That may look like the fan-out-on-write
  approach I just talked you out of, so let me draw the line I'm relying on. Involvement is a [1mfact[0m — "Dana commented on
  task 7" is true forever and never needs recomputing. Notification-worthiness is a [1mrule[0m — "people involved in a task
  should hear about status changes, unless muted" — and rules change. Approach A froze the rule; this freezes only the
  facts, and keeps every rule in the read query where it stays changeable. Maintaining the participants row at write
  time also turns the bell query into one indexed join instead of a scan across comments.

  [1mThe bell query[0m, roughly: events on tasks I participate in, newer than my read cursor, not authored by me, not
  individually dismissed — newest first, capped. Collapsing happens here too: consecutive events sharing task + actor +
  type inside a short window render as one entry.

  [1mOne assumption I need to name rather than smuggle in:[0m [3mAssumption: authentication is out of scope; stage one identifies
[0m  [3musers by a simple session-selected identity (a picker, not a login), validated by confirming with you before [0m
  [3mstage-one implementation.[0m Real auth is its own subsystem and would swallow this project. The schema above doesn't
  block adding it later — [38;5;153musers.id[39m is the only thing notifications touch.

  Does this section look right? In particular I want pushback on the participants-as-facts call and on deferring auth.

